46:15.820 --> 46:18.020
in theorems 8, 9, and 10

46:18.020 --> 46:19.160
which detail

46:19.160 --> 46:19.780
what they call

46:19.780 --> 46:20.860
the regenerative

46:20.860 --> 46:21.940
account of decline.

46:23.000 --> 46:24.380
Decline in a complex system

46:24.380 --> 46:25.460
rarely looks like

46:25.460 --> 46:26.880
a dramatic sudden explosion.

46:27.660 --> 46:28.120
It is usually

46:28.120 --> 46:29.680
a slow, subtle,

46:29.900 --> 46:31.180
bureaucratic contraction

46:31.180 --> 46:33.080
of your possible futures.

46:33.780 --> 46:34.460
How does the math

46:34.460 --> 46:35.300
actually map out

46:35.300 --> 46:35.880
that decline?

46:36.220 --> 46:36.960
Theorem 8 looks

46:36.960 --> 46:37.520
at the mechanics

46:37.520 --> 46:38.360
of what happens

46:38.360 --> 46:39.140
when a controller

46:39.140 --> 46:40.360
slowly adds more

46:40.360 --> 46:41.760
and more exclusions

46:41.760 --> 46:42.420
to the graph

46:42.420 --> 46:42.980
over time.

46:43.800 --> 46:44.340
The proof shows

46:44.340 --> 46:45.440
that adding exclusions

46:45.440 --> 46:46.920
strictly and irreversibly

46:46.920 --> 46:47.700
shrinks the set

46:47.700 --> 46:48.780
of corrective options

46:48.780 --> 46:49.620
available to you.

46:50.040 --> 46:50.680
Every time a new

46:50.680 --> 46:51.580
corporate policy

46:51.580 --> 46:52.080
is introduced

46:52.080 --> 46:52.560
that says

46:52.560 --> 46:53.680
